Drives its own private GC state (its own `gcBindMemory`/`gcInit`-bound arena),
exactly as `../gcspanfreelist` does, and every assertion is about that private
heap: how many chunks three successive same-shape bursts cost the reserve, and
whether the second and third are bounded by the first's -- the #4058 block
structure's whole point. Objects it allocates are the private collector's and
invisible to the ambient one.

CORRECTION: "None of it depends on whether the ambient (libbitrt) collector
ever runs" was true in spirit but understated — the file's earlier drafts of
this shape (see gcbit/#4160) went further and implied the ambient collector
effectively never fires. It does. Measured, aarch64-macos, 5 runs each policy:
default `collections=0` every time; `BIT_GC=stress` 72463, 72462, 72434,
72457, 72462 (denominator: 5/5, spread ~0.04%), well above stress.bit's
documented ~101 scheduler-loop floor. Confirmed default and `BIT_GC=stress`
stdout are both byte-identical to `.expected`.

Unlike ../chanmakeroots/../chanroots/../gcindexchurn/../gcreserve (#4184),
this program's own final assertion closes the rooting question directly
rather than relying on it being untested: `arena`/`roots` are read again at
the very end ("reached here so both stay rooted across every ambient
collection") and the result is printed as `rooted`, so the arena's live
interval genuinely extends through the last ambient collection this program
could see, and the printed value proves it did.
